output.text:
Now I have all the information needed to compile a comprehensive answer. Here are my findings:

---

## Summary

**DE2.0 does NOT have native hardware rotation (transpose x/y) at the mixer or blender level.** Rotation on R528 is achieved through two separate mechanisms: (1) a **software rotation framework** (`disp_rotation_sw`) that operates at the layer configuration level, and (2) a **G2D hardware-accelerated rotation** (`fb_g2d_rot`) that copies and rotates framebuffer contents through the separate G2D engine, not through the Display Engine itself.

---

## Finding 1: DE2.0 Hardware Registers Have No Rotation/Transpose Bit

The register definitions in `de_rtmx_type.h` are definitive -- width and height are always stored as independent 13-bit fields with no transpose flag.

**File:** `/data/vela/vendor/allwinnertech/chips/r528/drivers/rtos-hal/hal/source/disp2/disp/de/lowlevel_v2x/de_rtmx_type.h`

**Global size register (line 77-86):**
```c
union __glb_size_reg_t {
    unsigned int dwval;
    struct {
        unsigned int width:13;
        unsigned int r0:3;
        unsigned int height:13;
        unsigned int r1:3;
    } bits;
};
```

**Blend output size register (line 455-463):**
```c
union __bld_output_size_reg_t {
    unsigned int dwval;
    struct {
        unsigned int width:13;
        unsigned int r0:3;
        unsigned int height:13;
        unsigned int r1:3;
    } bits;
};
```

**Video overlay size register (line 193-201):**
```c
union __vi_ovl_size_reg_t {
    unsigned int dwval;
    struct {
        unsigned int ovl_width:13;
        unsigned int r0:3;
        unsigned int ovl_height:13;
        unsigned int r1:3;
    } bits;
};
```

The UI overlay, layer size, and pipe input size registers follow the same pattern -- always separate width/height fields with no transpose/swap/rotation bit anywhere in the entire register file. There is no `__glb_rotate_reg_t` or equivalent in the hardware.

